content.text:
The letterboxed mobile view looks like a wide band in the middle (390x219), with black top/bottom. Actually it's bigger than 219? It spans from ~310 to ~535 in the 844 screenshot = 225px, correct. The scene is undistorted and looks good: helipad H, path, palms, helicopter landed! The helicopter is visible on the pad (nice). The player is standing near the helipad with a red hat. The world band is quite small, but the layout is clean: HUD top (quest card, achievement toast), game band middle, objective toast, joystick and buttons bottom. This is a viable mobile layout, though the game view is only 27% of the screen height. A portrait player might prefer a bigger view.

Can we enlarge the game view while preserving aspect by making it, say, 100vw wide and placing controls overlaying the bottom of it? The band is 219px; we could crop less? No, 16:9 is fixed. We could increase it by using a 4:3 internal view for mobile, but then distortion? We can change the canvas aspect on mobile to 4:3 with CSS (390x292) and render a 4:3 viewport by adapting the game camera. That requires dynamic view but simpler: draw the world into 480x270 and stretch? No.

Could use a 4:3 band (390x292) by setting aspect-ratio:4/3 and rendering the game with a camera zoom-out horizontally? The canvas bitmap is 480x270 (16:9); displaying it at 4:3 would stretch (vertical 8%? 270/480=.5625 vs 3/4=.75 → 33% stretch). We could change the canvas backing to 480x360 and adjust all the drawing code (SW/SH) to 480x360; that's a moderate refactor: SW=480, SH=360, camera view 480x360, and re-tune UI coordinates/effects. The world view would be taller, showing more vertically; pixel art aspect correct. On desktop, the canvas would be 480x360 displayed at 1280x800 (16:10) with slight stretch (1.6 vs 1.33 → 20% horizontal stretch), unless letterboxed (object-fit contain) with side bars. Hmm.

We could set the internal resolution to 480x360 and use CSS `aspect-ratio:4/3` letterboxed on mobile → 390x292 band (slightly larger) but not much. To fill more vertical screen, use a square 1:1 internal (e.g., 480x480) → 390x390 band, nearly half the screen. That changes the game's FOV; the camera logic uses SW/SH constants. Refactoring SW/SH to 480x480 means the world view is 480x480; the camera clamps and rendering adapt, but all screen effects use SH; mostly fine. The UI is DOM, so unaffected. Desktop would use 480x480 (square) stretched onto 1280x800 unless letterboxed to 800x800 with side bars; not ideal.

We can choose the internal resolution dynamically based on aspect ratio, but that's more work. The current letterbox is acceptable and clean. Many mobile games are landscape-only; portrait users get a small view. Maybe add a "rotate" hint for a larger view. But the prompt demands touch controls; they work. I'll keep the letterbox and perhaps enlarge the band slightly by using a 15:9-ish crop? The internal 480x270 is 16:9; if displayed at 390x240 (aspect 1.625), it would stretch vertically ~9.6%, barely noticeable and gains 20px. Not worth.

Could move the game band upward to reduce the gap between HUD and game, and place the controls in the bottom black area. The current band is centered at 422, with HUD top and controls bottom; it looks balanced. Fine.

However, the achievement toast now sits at top 132, overlapping the quest card vertically? The quest card is at left top 76-220 and the achievement at right 132-190; they don't overlap horizontally. Good.

The objective toast at bottom 186 (y≈658) sits in the black gap between the band and controls; good.

The mobile HUD top now has a black background behind it (the page background), which looks intentional.

The quest card shows the chapter and description; good.

The top build: the stats bar sits at y≈77 across the width; the quest card starts at 76 and overlaps the stats bar horizontally? The quest card is at left 8, top 76; the stats bar is order 3 at the top, width 100%, spanning y 72-88; the quest card top 76 overlaps it! In the screenshot, the stats bar is above the quest card? I see stats "08:28 · CHUVA LEVE · 0040" at y≈78, and the quest card begins at y≈76 with its eyebrow "CAPÍTULO 01 · A CHEGADA" at y≈82, but the stats bar is drawn on top? The screenshot shows the quest card's eyebrow below the stats? Actually the quest card top is at 76, and the stats bar is at 74-88; they overlap, but the card's background may cover the stats, yet the stats text is visible across the full width above the card? In the screenshot, the stats line is at y 78, and the quest card starts at y 80 with a border; the stats appears above the card border because the card starts at 76 but its content begins at 88? There's overlap but visually the stats is in the gap between the brand row and the quest card. Let me look: the HUD top is flex-wrap; brand row y 8-60, actions right; stats row order 3 y 63-80. Quest card top 76 → its top border/eyebrow at 84, so the stats bar overlaps the top 4px of the card, but the card background is drawn below the stats? z-order: both inside #hud; `.quest-card` comes after `.hud-top` in DOM, so the card is on top, covering the stats bottom 4px. The screenshot shows stats fully visible, so maybe the card top is lower (the top 76 is the absolute position of the quest card, but the hud-top height pushes? No, absolute). The screenshot looks okay; don't worry.

The joystick and buttons are visible; good.

The game view being small is the main tradeoff. Alternative: overlay the game full-screen but with the camera aspect corrected via a dynamic render, which is more complex. Given the time, keep.

Wait, the user asked "Otimização para celulares" and "Controles touchscreen... funcionar perfeitamente em telas Android." The letterboxed view with controls is acceptable. But the game on a phone in portrait may feel too small. Could add a "GIRAR TELA" hint overlay in portrait suggesting landscape, and in landscape the game fills more (aspect 844/390=2.16 vs 1.78 → letterbox width? CSS aspect-ratio 16/9 with width 100% would letterbox vertically only slightly; in landscape, 844x475 band, good). Add a small hint in the menu footer for mobile: "Vire o celular para uma visão maior." That manages expectations.

Also, our CSS media query `max-width:820px` applies to landscape phones with width >820? A landscape phone is 844x390, width 844 > 820, so the touch UI detection `innerWidth<820` would be false and touch controls hidden! But `matchMedia('(pointer:coarse)')` sets touch-ui class on start; the media query `max-width:820px` for the canvas letterbox would not apply in landscape, so the canvas fills 844x390 (aspect 2.16) and stretches. Also the mobile CSS for HUD wouldn't apply. Many landscape phones are >820 CSS px wide in device pixels but with `viewport` scaling they report ~844. We should use `(pointer:coarse)` media query instead of max-width for the mobile styles, or set a larger breakpoint (e.g., 960px) to cover landscape. Use `@media(max-width:960px)` for the responsive UI and canvas? Desktop 1280 unaffected. A landscape phone 844x390 gets the mobile HUD (which is designed for portrait; in landscape it might be too large but okay) and letterboxing. Better: add a separate `@media (pointer:coarse)` block that applies the canvas letterbox and touch-specific UI, while the max-width:820 handles portrait. In landscape, the HUD desktop layout might work better (space), and the canvas full screen at 844x390 aspect 2.16 → stretch 21%. We can set the canvas aspect-ratio 16/9 also under `@media (pointer:coarse)`, centering it with width 100% height auto → 844x475, but the viewport height is 390, so it overflows vertically (band taller than screen) and gets clipped; not good. In landscape, letterbox horizontally: set canvas height 100%, width auto, aspect-ratio 16/9 → 693x390, centered with side bars. That preserves aspect and fills the height; controls overlay. Use CSS:
```css
@media (pointer:coarse){
  canvas#scene{width:auto;height:100%;aspect-ratio:16/9;left:50%;top:0;transform:translateX(-50%);inset:auto;}
}
```
But in portrait, this would make the canvas height 100% (844) and width 1500, cropping sides — not the desired letterbox. Need media orientation:
```css
@media (pointer:coarse) and (orientation:portrait){ canvas{inset:auto 0 auto 0;top:50%;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:16/9;transform:translateY(-50%)} }
